# fixtures/usage-log-contract.jsonl is a GOLDEN FILE: the checked-in contract
# between the orchestrator's usage-log writer (Python, record_usage) and the
# hosted server's reader (TypeScript, ingestUsageLog). Two suites in two
# languages assert against these exact bytes.
#
# core.autocrlf=true is set on the machine this was developed on, so without
# this line a fresh Windows clone would check the file out with CRLF endings
# while CI's ubuntu runner sees LF. Both readers happen to tolerate a trailing
# CR today (Python's splitlines() handles it; JSON.parse treats it as
# whitespace per RFC 8259), so this is insurance rather than a live fix — but a
# file whose whole purpose is byte-level agreement should not have its bytes
# depend on which OS cloned it.
fixtures/usage-log-contract.jsonl -text

# Shell scripts MUST check out with LF, on every platform.
#
# `docker/entrypoint.sh` is the container's ENTRYPOINT, and `compose.yaml`
# bind-mounts the repo at `/app` — which SHADOWS the copy the Dockerfile fixed
# with `sed -i 's/\r$//' && chmod +x`. So the file the container actually execs
# is the HOST checkout, and with `core.autocrlf=true` (set on the machine this
# was developed on) a fresh Windows clone gets `#!/bin/sh\r`: `docker compose up`
# then fails with a shebang error that names no cause.
#
# The whole-branch review found this, and it is invisible here because this
# working tree happens to hold the file with LF and the exec bit already set —
# the defect only appears on a clone. Two other layers back this up: the
# ENTRYPOINT now invokes the script through `sh`, so the exec bit cannot matter,
# and the file's index mode is `100755`.
*.sh text eol=lf
